  "body": "Given promises are the async primitive it might make more sense to implement streams as linked lists where the tail of each list node is a lazy promise for another list node. This would have a couple of practical advantages over the OO style of stream node.js uses in that multiple readers won't interfere with each other, so they are compose-able. And look-aheads aren't a hack. They ask for GC hooks a bit more forcefully though because you don't have a centralised object to call `.close()` on.\n\nRegardless though I think whatever design you come up with will be fine, I'm just wanting to get the idea on the table at the early stage (if it is early?) in case people like it. I've implementing them [here](//github.com/jkroso/result-stream) so if people want to have a play they can. I haven't used them seriously myself though so there not really vetted in JS AFAIK. The idea comes from [oz](//http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oz_%28programming_language%29) where they use data-flow variables in place of promises.\n",
